// Typecheck the mappings-pipeline sources (issue #25).
//
// `@tspml/mappings-pipeline` has a `test` script but no `build`, so nothing ever
// typechecked its `.mjs`. It is the tool that regenerates the symbol map on a game
// release — the highest-consequence code in the repo that no compiler was reading.
// A mistake here does not fail loudly; it produces a *plausible but wrong candidate
// map*, and the map is the thing every surface hash-gates against.
//
// Same non-strict posture as `tsconfig.smokes.json`: these are hand-written `.mjs`
// with JSDoc where it matters, and `strict` would demand annotations rather than
// find defects. See that file for the full reasoning.
//
// `webcrack` is not resolved here (`noResolve` is off but the import is untyped and
// `skipLibCheck` swallows its declarations) — the value is in checking OUR logic:
// property typos on the AST nodes we build, wrong arity on our own exported
// helpers, a `fs/promises` call misspelled.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2022",
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "lib": ["es2022"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  "include": [
    "../../tooling/mappings-pipeline/src/**/*.mjs",
    "../../tooling/mappings-pipeline/scripts/**/*.mjs"
  ],
  // The gitignored analysis cache holds ad-hoc probe scripts (and, transiently,
  // deobfuscated game source). Never typecheck it — it is not our code, and on a
  // developer machine it contains the game.
  "exclude": ["../../tooling/mappings-pipeline/.cache/**"]
}
